{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:26:09Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 177, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "diff --git a/docs/design.md b/docs/design.md\nindex 2bf0f3e..7743b51 100644\n--- a/docs/design.md\n+++ b/docs/design.md\n@@ -1,452 +1,52 @@\n-# Design: Give tasks a priority\n-\n-Source: Taiga #4 spec text as provided in this dispatch (see note below).\n-\n-> **Note on `docs/spec.md`:** at the time of writing, `docs/spec.md` on disk\n-> still holds the previous cycle's content (Taiga #3, \"Edit a task title\").\n-> The product-manager's Taiga #4 spec was included directly in this\n-> dispatch's \"What earlier stages produced\" section, pending the user's\n-> confirmation to overwrite `docs/spec.md`/`docs/design.md` and to resolve\n-> open question #1 (interleaved vs. full 3-tier sort). This design is built\n-> against that inline text, under the same \"interleaved \u2014 only `high`\n-> moves\" assumption the spec proceeds under. Recommend the orchestrator\n-> confirm both points with the user and persist `docs/spec.md` before the\n-> developer stage starts, so the developer isn't working from a stale file\n-> on disk.\n-\n-## Summary of decisions\n-\n-- **One control does both jobs.** Rather than a read-only badge plus a\n-  separate control to change priority (two elements fighting for space in\n-  an already-tight row \u2014 see round 2's `Edit`/`Done`/`Delete` row), each\n-  task gets a single native `<select>` \u2014 `priority-select` \u2014 that always\n-  shows the current priority as its own visible text (\"Low\"/\"Normal\"/\n-  \"High\") and lets the user change it in one action. This satisfies AC8\n-  (visible at a glance) and the \"control to change priority without\n-  entering the title-edit form\" requirement (spec open question 2) with no\n-  new UI pattern beyond what the app already has (a styled `<button>`- and\n-  `<input>`-adjacent form control).\n-- **Color only on the two priorities that deviate from baseline.** `normal`\n-  renders with the same neutral border/background every other control in\n-  this app uses (`#ccd2d6` border, white fill) \u2014 it's the default, so it\n-  shouldn't compete visually. `high` gets a solid warm-orange fill (new\n-  accent, not a reuse of `.error`'s red \u2014 see \"Why not reuse the error\n-  red\" below). `low` gets a light neutral-gray fill, visually recessive.\n-  This means scanning the list, only `high` rows pull the eye, which is\n-  literally the ask (\"Let me... see that at a glance\").\n-- **Why not reuse the error red (`#a3271d`) for `high`:** round 2's design\n-  established the rule \"one red = one meaning\" for this app \u2014 `#a3271d`\n-  means \"something is wrong.\" Reusing it for \"high priority\" would make a\n-  perfectly valid, successfully-saved task look like an error state.\n-  Introducing one new accent (orange, a different hue from red) keeps that\n-  semantic separation intact while still giving `high` a color no other\n-  element in the app currently uses.\n-- **Change applies immediately on select, no separate Save.** Matches this\n-  app's two existing precedents for single-field, low-risk actions:\n-  `TaskFilter`'s segmented control (click \u2192 immediately re-filters) and the\n-  `Done` button (click \u2192 immediately marks complete). A priority change is\n-  the same shape of action \u2014 one field, no risk of data loss, no reason to\n-  gate it behind a confirm step the way title editing is (title editing\n-  needs Save/Cancel because it's multi-character free text where a stray\n-  edit is easy to want to undo; a 3-option select isn't).\n-- **No optimistic update; follows the existing `run()` pattern exactly.**\n-  `App.jsx`'s `run()` helper (lines 33\u201340) awaits the API call, then\n-  `refresh()`s from the server, for every existing mutation (add, complete,\n-  delete, edit-title). Priority change is wired the same way \u2014 no new\n-  state-management pattern introduced. Practical effect: since the\n-  `<select>`'s `value` prop is bound to `task.priority` (server state, not\n-  local draft state), a failed change causes the control to simply snap\n-  back to its last-known-good value on the next render, with the existing\n-  global `.error` banner explaining why. No custom revert logic needed \u2014\n-  it falls out of the existing controlled-component wiring for free.\n-- **Sort is a stable partition applied after the existing filter step**, not\n-  a general sort. `high` tasks float to the top in their original relative\n-  order; everyone else (interleaved `normal`/`low`) keeps its original\n-  relative order below them \u2014 per the spec's explicit \"without disturbing\n-  the order of everything else\" and its open-question-1 assumption\n-  (interleaved, only `high` moves). JS's `Array.prototype.sort` has been a\n-  stable sort in every engine this project targets (Node/V8, all evergreen\n-  browsers) since ES2019, so a boolean partition key (`priority === 'high'\n-  ? 0 : 1`) is sufficient \u2014 no custom stable-sort implementation needed.\n-- **`AddTask` gets the same `priority-select`, defaulted to `normal`.**\n-  Reuses the exact same component/class as the row control rather than\n-  inventing a second priority-picking pattern for creation vs. editing.\n-\n-## Component tree change\n-\n-```\n-App.jsx\n-\u251c\u2500 AddTask.jsx\n-\u2502   [title input] [priority-select, default \"Normal\"] [Add]   \u2190 select is NEW\n-\u251c\u2500 TaskFilter.jsx (unchanged)\n-\u2514\u2500 TaskList.jsx\n-   \u2514\u2500 <li> per task (TaskRow)\n-       [title] [priority-select]  [Edit] [Done] [Delete]      \u2190 select is NEW\n-```\n-\n-No new files, no new component library. `priority-select` is a shared CSS\n-class (not a new React component) applied to a plain `<select>` in both\n-`AddTask.jsx` and `TaskList.jsx`'s `TaskRow`.\n-\n-## `AddTask.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n-\n-```jsx\n-const PRIORITIES = [\n-  { value: 'low', label: 'Low' },\n-  { value: 'normal', label: 'Normal' },\n-  { value: 'high', label: 'High' },\n-]\n-\n-export default function AddTask({ onAdd }) {\n-  const [title, setTitle] = useState('')\n-  const [priority, setPriority] = useState('normal')\n-\n-  function submit(e) {\n-    e.preventDefault()\n-    const trimmed = title.trim()\n-    if (!trimmed) return\n-    onAdd(trimmed, priority)\n-    setTitle('')\n-    setPriority('normal')\n-  }\n-\n-  return (\n-    <form className=\"add-task\" onSubmit={submit}>\n-      <input\n-        value={title}\n-        onChange={(e) => setTitle(e.target.value)}\n-        placeholder=\"What needs doing?\"\n-        aria-label=\"Task title\"\n-      />\n-      <select\n-        className={`priority-select priority-${priority}`}\n-        value={priority}\n-        onChange={(e) => setPriority(e.target.value)}\n-        aria-label=\"Priority\"\n-      >\n-        {PRIORITIES.map((p) => <option key={p.value} value={p.value}>{p.label}</option>)}\n-      </select>\n-      <button type=\"submit\" disabled={!title.trim()}>Add</button>\n-    </form>\n-  )\n-}\n-```\n-\n-`PRIORITIES` (value/label pairs) mirrors `TaskFilter.jsx`'s existing\n-`OPTIONS` shape exactly \u2014 same pattern, new data.\n-\n-## `TaskList.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n-\n-Add a `priority-select` to `TaskRow`'s **view-mode** row only (edit mode \u2014\n-the title-editing form \u2014 is unaffected; priority stays visible and\n-editable independently while a title edit is in progress, matching the\n-spec's precedent that title-edit already leaves other fields alone):\n-\n-```jsx\n-const PRIORITIES = [\n-  { value: 'low', label: 'Low' },\n-  { value: 'normal', label: 'Normal' },\n-  { value: 'high', label: 'High' },\n-]\n-\n-// inside TaskRow(), view-mode return, before the Edit button:\n-<select\n-  className={`priority-select priority-${task.priority}`}\n-  value={task.priority}\n-  onChange={(e) => onPriorityChange(task.id, e.target.value)}\n-  aria-label={`Priority for ${task.title}`}\n->\n-  {PRIORITIES.map((p) => <option key={p.value} value={p.value}>{p.label}</option>)}\n-</select>\n-```\n-\n-`onPriorityChange` is a new prop threaded through `TaskList` \u2192\n-`TaskRow`, alongside the existing `onComplete`/`onDelete`/`onUpdate`\n-(`TaskList.jsx:9`, `:19-21`).\n-\n-`PRIORITIES` is duplicated between `AddTask.jsx` and `TaskList.jsx` as\n-written above (2 short arrays, 3 entries each) rather than factored into a\n-shared constants file \u2014 three lines twice is not worth a new module in a\n-project this size; if a third consumer shows up, that's the point to\n-factor it, not before.\n-\n-## `api.js` change\n-\n-```js\n-export const setPriority = (id, priority) =>\n-  request(`/api/tasks/${id}/priority`, { method: 'PATCH', body: JSON.stringify({ priority }) })\n-```\n-\n-Matches the existing `completeTask`/`updateTask` call shape\n-(`api.js:19-21`). `createTask` extends to also send `priority` in its\n-body (exact signature is the developer's call \u2014 mirrors how `updateTask`\n-already takes `(id, title)`).\n-\n-## `App.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n-\n-```jsx\n-const visibleTasks = tasks.filter((t) => {\n-  if (filter === 'open') return !t.done\n-  if (filter === 'done') return t.done\n-  return true\n-})\n-\n-const sortedTasks = [...visibleTasks].sort(\n-  (a, b) => (a.priority === 'high' ? 0 : 1) - (b.priority === 'high' ? 0 : 1)", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_017KfJe8JRXaiAsBNnVyhvik", "session_id": "06a04b86-59b4-4030-b9ec-a0f1ea773699"}}
